.\" timeout.1 -- maux's timeout(1), in the V7 man(1) shape. An
.\" original page (this project's own text, not copied from a
.\" historic timeout(1)), so it carries no third-party notice --
.\" MIT like the code, same posture as env.1. Rendered at build
.\" time to build/man/TIMEOUT.TXT and staged on the play image as
.\" /usr/man/TIMEOUT: `more /usr/man/TIMEOUT`.
.TH TIMEOUT 1 "September 15, 2026" maux
.SH NAME
timeout \- run a utility, kill it if it outruns a deadline
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B timeout
[
.B \-fp
] [
.B \-k
.I time
] [
.B \-s
.I signal
]
.I duration utility
[
.I argument
] ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Timeout
runs
.I utility
and waits up to
.I duration
seconds for it to exit. If it has not exited by then,
.I signal
(default TERM) is sent to it, and
.I timeout
exits 124 once it is reaped.
.I Duration
and
.I time
are decimal integers with an optional trailing
.BR s ,
.BR m ,
or
.B h
suffix (default seconds).
.PP
.I Utility
is resolved against
.B PATH
exactly as a shell resolves a bare command word (execvp), and
its
.IR argv [0]
is the word as typed, not the resolved path.
.TP
.B \-f
Foreground: accepted and does nothing. There is no process-group
concept on this machine for it to skip.
.TP
.B \-k \fItime\fR
If
.I signal
alone does not reap
.I utility
within
.I time
more seconds, send it KILL as well.
.TP
.B \-p
Preserve status: exit with
.IR utility 's
own status even after a timeout, instead of 124.
.TP
.B \-s \fIsignal\fR
Send
.I signal
instead of TERM. A decimal number or one of the names TERM,
KILL, HUP, INT, QUIT.
.SH FILES
/usr/man/TIMEOUT  this page
.SH "SEE ALSO"
sh(1), kill(1), nohup(1), sleep(1)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
If
.I utility
was invoked, its own exit status, unless it timed out and
.B \-p
was not given, in which case 124. Otherwise: 125 on a bad
.I duration
or option, or if fewer than two operands remain after options;
126 if
.I utility
was found but could not be run; 127 if it could not be found on
.BR PATH .
.SH BUGS
There is no interval timer on this machine: the deadline is a
tick count sampled and polled, backed by an AppleMouse VBL or
Mockingboard tick source. A machine with neither refuses with a
diagnostic (exit 125) rather than hanging. A fractional
.I duration
(such as
.BR 1.5 )
is refused rather than truncated or rounded.
